"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkn9e_site||=[]).push([[8130],{77735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-05-grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-05-grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager/index.md","title":"Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager: rules, routing and coexistence","description":"Compare Grafana Alerting with Prometheus and Alertmanager: rule ownership, data sources, notification routing, coexistence and a practical way to choose.","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."},{"inline":false,"label":"Grafana","permalink":"/blog/tags/grafana","description":"Grafana Alerting, data sources and notification workflows."}],"readingTime":6.59,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager","title":"Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager: rules, routing and coexistence","description":"Compare Grafana Alerting with Prometheus and Alertmanager: rule ownership, data sources, notification routing, coexistence and a practical way to choose.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["prometheus","alerting","grafana"],"image":"/img/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"SQL query alerting: monitor failed orders in MySQL","permalink":"/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql"}},"content":"![Two alerting paths: Prometheus rules and Grafana-managed rules can both send alerts to an external Alertmanager](/img/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager.webp)\\n\\n**Choose Prometheus with Alertmanager when your rules belong with your metrics and are maintained as code. Choose Grafana-managed alerting when you need Grafana to evaluate queries across supported data sources and manage conditions through its UI or provisioning tools.** You can also keep Prometheus rules, add Grafana-managed rules for other data, and use the same external Alertmanager for notification delivery.\\n\\nThe important decision is where a rule runs and who owns its notifications. Here is how to make those choices without evaluating the same condition twice.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What you are comparing\\n\\nPrometheus evaluates PromQL alerting rules. When an expression produces a matching label set, Prometheus tracks that alert\'s state and, once its pending period has elapsed, sends a firing alert downstream. **Alertmanager handles the delivery side:** deduplication, grouping, routing, silences and inhibition. It does not query your metrics or evaluate the PromQL threshold. See the official [alerting rules](https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/alerting_rules/) and [Alertmanager overview](https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/).\\n\\nGrafana Alerting includes a rule evaluator and notification management. With a **Grafana-managed rule**, Grafana executes the queries, evaluates the condition and produces alert instances. Its notification configuration then decides where those alerts go. The data source must support backend alerting; a query that works on a dashboard is not sufficient proof. [Grafana documents that requirement here](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/alerting-rules/create-grafana-managed-rule/).\\n\\nTwo common paths therefore look like this:\\n\\n```text\\nPrometheus evaluates rules \u2192 Prometheus Alertmanager \u2192 receivers\\n\\nGrafana evaluates rules    \u2192 Grafana\'s Alertmanager   \u2192 contact points\\n```\\n\\nThe storage system can be the same Prometheus in both cases. Moving evaluation to Grafana does not require moving the stored metrics.\\n\\n## Grafana Alerting vs Prometheus + Alertmanager\\n\\n| Decision | Prometheus + Alertmanager | Grafana-managed alerting |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Who evaluates the condition? | Prometheus | Grafana |\\n| What can the rule query? | Data accessible through PromQL | Supported backend data sources; rules can combine queries and expressions |\\n| Where is the rule maintained? | Prometheus rule files; commonly generated or deployed through GitOps | Grafana UI, provisioning files, Terraform or APIs |\\n| Who handles delivery? | External Alertmanager | Grafana\'s notification system, or a configured external Alertmanager |\\n| How are destinations selected? | Alertmanager routes and receivers | Contact points and notification policies, or the external Alertmanager\'s configuration |\\n| Can dashboards stay in Grafana? | Yes | Yes |\\n| Who diagnoses an evaluation failure? | The team operating Prometheus and its rule configuration | The team operating Grafana alert evaluation and its data source access |\\n\\nBoth designs can be managed as code. Grafana\'s UI is a useful option, and its [provisioning tools](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/set-up/provision-alerting-resources/) also support reviewable configuration. Decide who is allowed to edit provisioned resources and how those edits are deployed before treating either UI as the source of truth.\\n\\n## Three setups that are easy to confuse\\n\\n### 1. Grafana displays alerts that another system evaluates\\n\\nWith a **data source-managed rule**, a compatible external system stores and evaluates the rule. Grafana provides an interface to it. Grafana\'s documentation describes this separately from [Grafana-managed rules](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/alerting-rules/create-data-source-managed-rule/).\\n\\nCheck which API the data source exposes. A Mimir or Loki ruler, a plain Prometheus server and an Alertmanager do different jobs. Adding an Alertmanager data source does not give Grafana a general-purpose API for editing Prometheus rule files.\\n\\n### 2. Grafana evaluates rules and uses its own notifications\\n\\nGrafana owns both the evaluation and the delivery configuration. This fits a team that already operates Grafana, needs conditions over multiple supported sources, and wants one place to maintain those rules.\\n\\nFor each data source, verify the evaluator\'s credentials and network access. A dashboard viewed by a logged-in person and a background alert evaluation can have different access conditions.\\n\\n### 3. Grafana evaluates rules and sends them to an external Alertmanager\\n\\nThis is a useful coexistence model:\\n\\n```text\\nPrometheus rules \u2500\u2510\\n                 \u251c\u2192 external Alertmanager \u2192 existing receivers\\nGrafana rules \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\\n```\\n\\nIt lets a team keep its established routes and receivers while adding Grafana-managed evaluation. Grafana documents two ways to configure this: [forward all Grafana-managed alerts to an enabled Alertmanager, or use an Alertmanager contact point for selected alerts](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/set-up/configure-alertmanager/). Adding the data source alone does not enable forwarding.\\n\\nKeep the route labels consistent. For example, both evaluators might attach `team=\\"payments\\"` and `env=\\"prod\\"`, so an existing payments route can match either source. The alert names should still describe the conditions distinctly.\\n\\n## A small example: who detects the outage?\\n\\nHere is a Prometheus rule that checks whether a scraped target has been unreachable for five minutes:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngroups:\\n  - name: service-availability\\n    rules:\\n      - alert: ServiceTargetDown\\n        expr: up{job=\\"checkout\\"} == 0\\n        for: 5m\\n        labels:\\n          severity: warning\\n          team: payments\\n        annotations:\\n          summary: \\"Checkout target is unreachable\\"\\n```\\n\\nPrometheus evaluates `up`, owns the five-minute pending state and sends the alert with the `team` label. Alertmanager can then route it to the payments receiver.\\n\\nIf you recreate the condition as a Grafana-managed rule, Grafana becomes a second evaluator. That may be appropriate during a controlled comparison, but leaving both enabled creates two independent alert identities. Sharing a destination does not guarantee they will deduplicate.\\n\\nAlso distinguish **zero** from **missing**. This expression matches a target whose `up` value is zero. It does not match a target that has disappeared from discovery and no longer has a current series. Test that case separately. Grafana-managed rules also have explicit [No Data and Error handling](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/fundamentals/alert-rule-evaluation/nodata-and-error-states/), which should be reviewed when translating a rule.\\n\\n## Can Grafana edit Alertmanager routes?\\n\\nThe answer depends on the Alertmanager implementation. For a **Prometheus Alertmanager**, Grafana can manage silences, but contact points, notification policies and templates are read-only in that integration: its HTTP API does not support updating those resources. Edit and deploy the external configuration through the system that owns it. [Grafana calls out this limitation](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/alerting/set-up/configure-alertmanager/).\\n\\nThis matters when the original requirement is simply \u201cgive us a UI.\u201d Viewing an alert, adding a silence, editing a routing tree and authoring a detection rule are four separate capabilities. Check which one your team needs before changing the architecture.\\n\\n## How to choose\\n\\n- **Keep Prometheus evaluation** when the rules already live alongside instrumentation, changes go through code review, and PromQL covers the detection requirements. Use Grafana for dashboards and Alertmanager for delivery.\\n- **Choose Grafana-managed evaluation** when a team needs its query and expression workflow over supported data sources, and is prepared to operate Grafana as an alerting dependency.\\n- **Use both with an external Alertmanager** when evaluation belongs in different systems but notification ownership should remain centralized. Document which system evaluates each condition.\\n\\nStart a migration with one non-critical rule. Compare its labels, firing time, recovery behavior and destination against the existing rule. Include an empty result and a failed query in the check. Then disable the old evaluator for that condition and verify that only the intended notification path remains.\\n\\nHigh availability needs a separate review. A working dashboard does not demonstrate that evaluation and delivery will survive a failed instance. Our [Prometheus HA guide](/blog/prometheus-high-availability) separates storage redundancy from alerting redundancy.\\n\\n## Where Nightingale fits\\n\\nNightingale is another evaluation option when rules need business-group ownership and a UI across metrics, logs and SQL sources. Its [Grafana Alerting comparison](/comparisons/grafana-alerting) and [Prometheus + Alertmanager comparison](/comparisons/prometheus-alertmanager) describe the relevant trade-offs. For a database example, see [SQL query alerting on failed MySQL orders](/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql).\\n\\nFor the wider choice, the [open-source alerting platform guide](/comparisons/open-source-alerting-platforms) separates rule evaluators, aggregation layers and on-call tools. Choose the layer that solves the operational requirement first."},{"id":"sql-query-alerting-mysql","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-05-sql-query-alerting-mysql/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-05-sql-query-alerting-mysql/index.md","title":"SQL query alerting: monitor failed orders in MySQL","description":"Build a MySQL query alert for failed orders with Nightingale: a bounded SQL window, numeric result fields, stable labels, recovery behavior and query-cost checks.","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"SQL","permalink":"/blog/tags/sql","description":"Alerting on business data with SQL queries."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."}],"readingTime":7.1,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sql-query-alerting-mysql","title":"SQL query alerting: monitor failed orders in MySQL","description":"Build a MySQL query alert for failed orders with Nightingale: a bounded SQL window, numeric result fields, stable labels, recovery behavior and query-cost checks.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["sql","alerting"],"image":"/img/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager: rules, routing and coexistence","permalink":"/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager"},"nextItem":{"title":"How long should a Prometheus alert wait? Data from 692 rules","permalink":"/blog/prometheus-alert-for-duration"}},"content":"![A MySQL order-outcomes table feeds a five-minute count query, a threshold and a notification](/img/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql.webp)\\n\\n**SQL query alerting runs a query on a schedule and checks a numeric result against a condition.** It is useful when the number you care about already lives in a business database: failed orders, overdue jobs or a queue that has stopped draining.\\n\\nThis example counts failed orders in MySQL over the last five minutes and configures Nightingale to alert when that count stays at five or more for two minutes. It also handles the quiet case: no failed orders should produce a numeric zero that lets the alert recover.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Decide what a failed order means\\n\\nFor this example, an application writes one final outcome per order into `order_outcomes`. The row is immutable after completion. `completed_at` is a UTC `DATETIME(6)`, and `status` is either `paid` or `failed`.\\n\\nHere is a minimal schema for a scratch database:\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE TABLE order_outcomes (\\n    order_id BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\\n    channel VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL,\\n    status VARCHAR(16) NOT NULL,\\n    completed_at DATETIME(6) NOT NULL,\\n    INDEX alert_window (completed_at, status, channel)\\n);\\n```\\n\\nIf your application records individual payment attempts, counting rows would count attempts rather than orders. If it updates the same order from failed to paid after a retry, the result measures current state rather than historical failures. Choose the table and timestamp that match the question you want the alert to answer.\\n\\nThe explicit UTC convention matters. MySQL `DATETIME` does not perform the session time-zone conversion that `TIMESTAMP` does. This example therefore compares it with `UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)`. Adapt both sides together if your application uses a different convention. [MySQL explains the type behavior here](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/datetime.html).\\n\\n## Write a query that also represents zero\\n\\n```sql\\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS failed_orders\\nFROM order_outcomes\\nWHERE status = \'failed\'\\n  AND completed_at >= UTC_TIMESTAMP(6) - INTERVAL 5 MINUTE\\n  AND completed_at < UTC_TIMESTAMP(6);\\n```\\n\\nThe lower bound keeps the scan within the recent window. The upper bound excludes future-dated rows. MySQL\'s current-time functions are evaluated consistently within a statement, so the two boundaries describe one window. See the [date and time function reference](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/date-and-time-functions.html).\\n\\nMost importantly, `COUNT(*)` without `GROUP BY` returns one row even when nothing matches:\\n\\n| failed_orders |\\n|---:|\\n| 0 |\\n\\nThat is a successful observation of zero failures. A query error or an absent result is a different condition. [MySQL documents the empty-input behavior of COUNT here](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/aggregate-functions.html#function_count).\\n\\n## Connect the database and map the result\\n\\nIn Nightingale, register a MySQL data source with an account that has `SELECT` access to the required table. Set its associated alerting engine cluster, connection limits and timeout. The [SQL database setup guide](/docs/data/sql-databases) covers those fields.\\n\\nCreate a MySQL alert rule, select that data source, and put the statement above in query **A**. Under **Auxiliary settings**, set:\\n\\n| Setting | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Value field | `failed_orders` |\\n| Label field | Empty: this query returns only the numeric column |\\n| Trigger expression | `$A.failed_orders >= 5` |\\n| Execution frequency | `@every 60s` as a starting point |\\n| For duration | `120` seconds |\\n| Recovery configuration | Recover only when data exists and the trigger condition is not met |\\n\\nThe threshold and schedule are example settings, not recommended limits for every service. With a 60-second schedule and a 120-second pending period, detection requires several consecutive matching evaluations. It is not an exact timer starting at the fifth failed order. The [evaluation and recovery guide](/docs/alerting/evaluation) explains the timing.\\n\\nUse **Preview** on the rule\'s query card to check the value. In Nightingale\'s open-source edition, this MySQL workflow is in the rule form rather than the metrics explorer. Then use **Test fire** to check the expression and inspect [evaluation records](/docs/alerting/execution-records) after saving.\\n\\nWrite the time predicate into the SQL itself. Nightingale\'s MySQL alert query does not supply Grafana-style `$__timeFilter` macros with an evaluation time range. Changing a preview time picker is not a substitute for the two bounds in this statement. The complete field mapping is in [SQL-backed rules](/docs/alerting/sql-rules).\\n\\n## Add channels without losing recovery rows\\n\\nSuppose the payments team wants separate alerts for `card` and `bank_transfer`. A simple `GROUP BY channel` over failed rows stops returning a channel as soon as its failure count reaches zero. Under a data-required recovery policy, that missing row does not prove the alert has recovered.\\n\\nReturn the expected channel set and join the failures onto it:\\n\\n```sql\\nSELECT channels.channel,\\n       COUNT(outcomes.order_id) AS failed_orders\\nFROM (\\n    SELECT \'card\' AS channel\\n    UNION ALL\\n    SELECT \'bank_transfer\' AS channel\\n) AS channels\\nLEFT JOIN order_outcomes AS outcomes\\n  ON outcomes.channel = channels.channel\\n AND outcomes.status = \'failed\'\\n AND outcomes.completed_at >= UTC_TIMESTAMP(6) - INTERVAL 5 MINUTE\\n AND outcomes.completed_at < UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)\\nGROUP BY channels.channel;\\n```\\n\\nThe predicates stay in the `ON` clause, so a channel with no matches remains in the result. Count `outcomes.order_id`, not `COUNT(*)`: the latter would count the unmatched left-join row as one.\\n\\nNow set **Value field** to `failed_orders` and **Label field** to `channel`. Each channel becomes a separate series with a stable identity. In an application with a canonical channels table, use that instead of the two literal rows. Keep the expected set complete so new channels are not silently excluded.\\n\\nAvoid order IDs, error text and timestamps as labels. They change on every evaluation or produce a series per order, while the operational question here is a count per channel.\\n\\n## What recovery says about the business\\n\\nWhen the query returns a count below five, the trigger condition no longer holds. With the recovery policy above and no additional recovery delay, the alert can recover on that successful evaluation.\\n\\nThis means **fewer than five failures remain in the rolling five-minute window**. It does not prove the underlying problem has been fixed. Old failures eventually age out even if no new orders arrive. If a broken checkout stops producing outcomes entirely, this particular count will also fall to zero. Monitor stalled processing or outcome freshness separately when that failure mode matters.\\n\\nLikewise, a database connection failure must not be interpreted as a zero. Keeping an existing incident open when its result is unavailable helps, but it does not by itself create a new incident for every query failure. Monitor the query\'s execution status and the alerting system\'s health too.\\n\\n## Check the cost of repeated queries\\n\\nThe example index begins with `completed_at`, allowing MySQL to consider a range over recent outcomes. Check the actual plan using [`EXPLAIN`](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/using-explain.html) and measure it against representative data. An index that works on a small scratch table may not be the best one for your production distribution.\\n\\nThree details matter when the query runs continuously:\\n\\n- **A one-row result can still scan millions of rows.** The data source\'s output row limit does not cap the work of `COUNT(*)`.\\n- **The evaluator repeats the cost.** Budget for the number of rules, data sources and evaluation cycles, and inspect the actual evaluation duration.\\n- **A reporting replica can be stale.** If you query a replica, account for replication lag and choose a detection window that matches the freshness you can observe.\\n\\nIf a bounded query is still expensive, aggregate outcomes into a small reporting table or export a counter through your metrics pipeline. The [symptom-based alerting guide](/blog/symptom-based-alerting) helps choose which business failure deserves a page.\\n\\n## Check firing, recovery and delivery separately\\n\\nBefore enabling the rule for a production team, use a scratch data source to verify these cases:\\n\\n| Input | Expected result |\\n|---|---|\\n| No recent failures | Global query returns `0`; channel query returns a zero for each expected channel |\\n| Five or more recent failures | Count crosses the threshold; an event appears after the configured pending period |\\n| Only old or future-dated failures | Excluded from this window |\\n| One channel becomes healthy | Its row remains present with a below-threshold count |\\n| Connection or SQL error | Visible as an execution failure, not a healthy zero |\\n\\nFinally attach a [notification rule](/docs/notifications/rules) with the appropriate severity and destination. A successful query, a firing event and a received notification are three separate checks. Use [Test fire](/docs/alerting/test-fire) for evaluation and the notification rule\'s own test for delivery.\\n\\nIf you are choosing where to run these rules, [Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager](/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager) explains evaluation and notification ownership. Nightingale\'s [SQL rule guide](/docs/alerting/sql-rules) covers multiple value columns, joins and the other SQL-backed data sources."},{"id":"prometheus-alert-for-duration","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/prometheus-alert-for-duration","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-alert-for-duration/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-alert-for-duration/index.md","title":"How long should a Prometheus alert wait? Data from 692 rules","description":"We counted the for duration and severity of 692 alert rule templates. 44% use the 60s default. Here is when for: 0, 60s, 15m and 1h are the right choice.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."},{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."},{"inline":false,"label":"Data","permalink":"/blog/tags/data","description":"Posts built on numbers from Nightingale\'s own templates and rule library."}],"readingTime":8.18,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"prometheus-alert-for-duration","title":"How long should a Prometheus alert wait? Data from 692 rules","description":"We counted the for duration and severity of 692 alert rule templates. 44% use the 60s default. Here is when for: 0, 60s, 15m and 1h are the right choice.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["alerting","prometheus","data"],"image":"/img/blog/alert-for-duration.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SQL query alerting: monitor failed orders in MySQL","permalink":"/blog/sql-query-alerting-mysql"},"nextItem":{"title":"kube-state-metrics vs metrics-server: which should you use?","permalink":"/blog/kube-state-metrics-vs-metrics-server-vs-cadvisor-vs-node-exporter"}},"content":"![A histogram of for durations across 692 alert rule templates, with 304 rules at 60 seconds](/img/blog/alert-for-duration.webp)\\n\\nEvery alert rule answers two questions: what counts as bad, and how long it has to stay bad. Most review effort goes into the first one, the expression and the threshold. The second is the `for` duration, and it often gets whatever value the tool filled in.\\n\\nWe wanted to know how often that happens, so we counted. Nightingale ships alert rule templates for 49 integrations. **692 rules, and 304 of them (44%) have a `for` duration of exactly 60 seconds**, which is the default in Nightingale\'s rule editor. This post walks through what the numbers show and what `for` value suits which kind of alert.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What `for` does\\n\\nAn alert rule is evaluated on a schedule. When the expression first returns a series, the alert is *pending*. It becomes *firing* only once the same series has kept matching on every evaluation for at least the `for` duration. If the series disappears for even one evaluation, the clock starts over.\\n\\nThree consequences follow:\\n\\n- **Detection time is about `for` plus one evaluation interval.** A rule with `for: 5m` evaluated every minute fires five to six minutes after the problem starts.\\n- **A series that flaps never fires.** If the condition comes and goes faster than `for`, no alert is created, and no recovery either. That is the point of `for`, and also a risk.\\n- **The defaults differ by tool.** In Prometheus, leaving out `for` means 0, so the alert fires on the first evaluation that matches. In Nightingale\'s rule editor, a new rule starts at a 60-second `for` and a 60-second evaluation interval. The bundled templates set their own interval, almost always 15 or 30 seconds.\\n\\n## How we counted\\n\\nThe data is the alert rule templates bundled with Nightingale\'s integrations: Kubernetes, Linux, MySQL, Redis, Elasticsearch and 44 more. They are published in the [alert rule library](/alert-rules) and on each integration page.\\n\\n- 705 rules in total, **692 after removing exact duplicates** (same integration, same name, same expression).\\n- Some checks exist twice on purpose, once for Categraf metrics and once for a Prometheus exporter, because the metric names differ. We count those separately.\\n- These are **templates**, not production configs. They show what template authors chose. They say nothing about what users changed after importing them.\\n- Counted on 2026-11-01, after the two changes described in Finding 4.\\n\\n## The distribution\\n\\n| `for` | Rules | Share |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 0 | 51 | 7.4% |\\n| 60s | 304 | 43.9% |\\n| 2m | 59 | 8.5% |\\n| 3m | 67 | 9.7% |\\n| 5m | 86 | 12.4% |\\n| 10m | 38 | 5.5% |\\n| 15m | 58 | 8.4% |\\n| Over 15m | 26 | 3.8% |\\n\\nThree rules use 15s or 90s and are left out of the table.\\n\\n## Finding 1: the default wins, unless someone chose deliberately\\n\\nThe 60-second spike is the obvious feature. We can\'t tell from the data whether 304 authors thought about it and picked 60s, or simply kept what the form offered. What we can see is a clear difference between where the rules came from:\\n\\n- **86 rules** in the Kubernetes template keep the standard alert names from [kubernetes-mixin](https://github.com/kubernetes-monitoring/kubernetes-mixin) and the Prometheus monitoring mixins, the community rule sets behind most Kubernetes monitoring stacks. **46 of them use 15 minutes**, and only 2 use 60 seconds. The mixin authors set `for` alert by alert.\\n- Rules written for Categraf and for Prometheus exporters have a **median of 60 seconds**. Only 4 of the 352 use 15 minutes.\\n\\nNeither is wrong as a whole. The point is that a default is a choice someone else made for you, before they knew what the rule would watch. With the templates\' 15- or 30-second evaluation intervals, a 60-second `for` means \\"matched on two to four evaluations in a row\\". That is reasonable for CPU, memory and latency. It is too short for things that recover on their own, and too long for events that are over in seconds.\\n\\n## Finding 2: `for: 0` is right for events, if the window is wide enough\\n\\n51 rules fire on the first match (four of them are host heartbeat checks with no PromQL). Most of the rest watch something that has *already happened*, so waiting adds nothing:\\n\\n- A counter went up: `increase(node_vmstat_oom_kill[1m]) > 0`. The OOM kill happened; it will not un-happen in five minutes.\\n- A deadline is close: `kubelet_certificate_manager_client_ttl_seconds < 604800`. Under seven days is under seven days.\\n- A process restarted: `mysql_global_status_uptime < 60`.\\n\\nThe restart check hides a trap. `uptime < 60` is only true for the first 60 seconds after a restart. Any `for` of 60 seconds or more means the alert can never fire, because uptime passes 60 before the duration is met. And the window has to fit several evaluations: copy `uptime < 60` into a rule evaluated every 60 seconds, and it gets one chance per restart, which scrape delay can easily push out of the window.\\n\\nThe templates get this right in different ways. The MySQL and MongoDB rules use `uptime < 60` with `for: 0`, and they evaluate every 15 seconds, so each restart gets about four chances. The Redis rules widen the window to `uptime_in_seconds < 600`. The Apache, EMQX and OpenLDAP rules use `uptime < 300` with `for: 60s`. **Make the window several evaluation intervals wide, keep `for` well under it, and keep both in mind if you change the evaluation interval.**\\n\\n## Finding 3: some rules move the waiting into the expression\\n\\nOf the 47 `for: 0` rules that have a PromQL expression, 22 wrap their metric in a range function, so the persistence check happens inside PromQL:\\n\\n```promql\\n# GitLab\'s ServiceDown template, for: 0\\navg_over_time(up[5m]) * 100 < 50\\n```\\n\\nThis is not the same as `up == 0` with `for: 5m`:\\n\\n| | `up == 0` with `for: 5m` | `avg_over_time(up[5m]) < 0.5` with `for: 0` |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Fires when | Every evaluation in the last 5 minutes saw the target down | The target was down for more than half of the last 5 minutes |\\n| One good scrape in the middle | Resets the clock | Barely moves the average |\\n| Good for | \\"Continuously broken\\" | \\"Mostly broken\\", for flapping targets |\\n\\nPick the one that matches what you mean. A target that answers one scrape in four is broken, and only the second form will say so.\\n\\n## Finding 4: a few instant checks deserved a longer wait\\n\\nCounting also turned up `for: 0` rules that compare a sampled value against a threshold with no window at all. Two of them we changed while writing this post:\\n\\n- `elasticsearch_cluster_health_status{color=\\"yellow\\"} == 1`. A cluster goes yellow on every rolling restart while replicas are reallocated. It now waits 10 minutes.\\n- `(node_nf_conntrack_entries / node_nf_conntrack_entries_limit) > 0.75`. A single busy scrape was enough to trigger it. It now waits 5 minutes, the same as the sibling rule at 80%.\\n\\nThe tables in this post already include both changes. The general rule: give sampled gauges a few evaluation intervals, or a range function as in Finding 3. Keep `for: 0` for the checks that really are binary: `mysql_up == 0`, a failed systemd unit, a replication thread that stopped.\\n\\n## Finding 5: long waits are for slow problems\\n\\n26 rules wait longer than 15 minutes. Most of them watch something that develops slowly:\\n\\n- The node filesystem predictions from kubernetes-mixin use one hour. A `predict_linear` forecast swings a lot on short windows; an hour of consistent prediction means the trend is real.\\n- Prometheus TSDB compaction failures wait four hours. One failed compaction is noise; a run of them is a problem.\\n- A kernel version mismatch across nodes waits six hours. It is a hygiene check, not an incident.\\n- The slow windows of the Kubernetes API error budget burn alerts wait one and three hours. They are designed to catch slow, steady burns.\\n\\nLong `for` values pair naturally with a lower severity. Nobody should be paged at 3 a.m. for something that took six hours to become true.\\n\\n## The severity side\\n\\nThe same templates carry a severity. Nightingale has three levels: S1 (critical), S2 (warning) and S3 (info).\\n\\n| Severity | Rules | Share |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| S2 / warning | 490 | 71% |\\n| S1 / critical | 137 | 20% |\\n| S3 / info | 60 | 9% |\\n\\nFive rules have no rule-level severity. When seven rules in ten share a level, the level stops helping anyone route or prioritize. A workable split:\\n\\n- **S1**: someone must act now. Users are affected or data is about to be lost.\\n- **S2**: someone should look today. Capacity trends, single replicas down with redundancy left.\\n- **S3**: record it, don\'t notify. Useful context during an incident.\\n\\n24 templates put several severities on one rule. The Linux CPU rule, for example, uses `cpu_usage_active > 75` as S3, `> 85` as S2 and `> 95` as S1. One rule with three tiers keeps the thresholds side by side, which is easier to review than three separate rules that drift apart.\\n\\n## Rules of thumb\\n\\n| What the rule watches | `for` | Example |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Something that already happened | 0 | OOM kill counter increased, certificate under 7 days |\\n| A binary up/down state | 0 to 1 evaluation interval | `mysql_up == 0` |\\n| A sampled gauge: CPU, memory, latency, error rate | 2\u20133 evaluation intervals | CPU over 90% |\\n| Something that often recovers by itself | Longer than its normal recovery time | A queue that usually drains in 5 minutes: 10m |\\n| A slow trend | 30m to several hours, lower severity | Disk full within 24 hours |\\n\\n`for` only controls when an alert starts. If an alert fires and recovers over and over, `for` is not the fix. Use a recovery delay instead: `keep_firing_for` in Prometheus, or the recover duration in Nightingale. See [Evaluation interval and recovery](/docs/alerting/evaluation).\\n\\n## Where to look next\\n\\nEvery rule in the [alert rule library](/alert-rules) shows its severity and its `for` value (\\"Fires after\\"), and links to a runbook. You can download a whole rule set as a Prometheus rules file, or import it into Nightingale from the matching [integration](/integrations)."},{"id":"kube-state-metrics-vs-metrics-server-vs-cadvisor-vs-node-exporter","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/kube-state-metrics-vs-metrics-server-vs-cadvisor-vs-node-exporter","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-kubernetes-metrics-sources/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-kubernetes-metrics-sources/index.md","title":"kube-state-metrics vs metrics-server: which should you use?","description":"Compare kube-state-metrics and metrics-server, then see where cAdvisor and node_exporter fit for autoscaling, resource monitoring and Kubernetes alerts.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/blog/tags/kubernetes","description":"Monitoring and alerting on Kubernetes clusters."},{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."}],"readingTime":8.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"kube-state-metrics-vs-metrics-server-vs-cadvisor-vs-node-exporter","title":"kube-state-metrics vs metrics-server: which should you use?","description":"Compare kube-state-metrics and metrics-server, then see where cAdvisor and node_exporter fit for autoscaling, resource monitoring and Kubernetes alerts.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["kubernetes","prometheus","alerting"],"image":"/img/blog/kubernetes-metrics-sources.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How long should a Prometheus alert wait? Data from 692 rules","permalink":"/blog/prometheus-alert-for-duration"},"nextItem":{"title":"MCP server for alerting: six design decisions","permalink":"/blog/mcp-server-for-alerting"}},"content":"![Four Kubernetes metric sources: kube-state-metrics, cAdvisor, node_exporter and metrics-server](/img/blog/kubernetes-metrics-sources.webp)\\n\\n**kube-state-metrics exposes Kubernetes object state for monitoring; metrics-server supplies recent CPU and memory usage through the Metrics API for autoscaling and `kubectl top`.** A Deployment\'s missing replicas and a Pod\'s current CPU usage are different questions. A cluster that needs both autoscaling and object-state alerts will commonly use both components.\\n\\nFor a full monitoring setup, add the other two sources: cAdvisor for container resource usage and node_exporter for the host underneath. This post explains which component answers each question, where to scrape it, and the metric renames that can break an otherwise valid alert query.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The short answer\\n\\n| Component | Measures | Where it gets the data | Metric prefix | Use it for |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **kube-state-metrics** | The state of Kubernetes objects: desired vs actual replicas, pod phase, container restarts, resource requests and limits | Watches the API server | `kube_` | Alerting and dashboards |\\n| **cAdvisor** | What each container actually uses: CPU, memory, throttling, filesystem, network | Built into the kubelet, served at `/metrics/cadvisor` | `container_` | Alerting and dashboards |\\n| **node_exporter** | The machine underneath: CPU modes, memory, disks, filesystems, network interfaces | A DaemonSet reading `/proc` and `/sys` on each node | `node_` | Alerting and dashboards |\\n| **metrics-server** | A CPU and memory snapshot for each pod and node | Scrapes the kubelet\'s `/metrics/resource` | none; it serves the Metrics API (`metrics.k8s.io`) | `kubectl top`, the Horizontal and Vertical Pod Autoscalers |\\n\\nYou will also meet two more sources. The kubelet\'s own `/metrics` endpoint provides things like `kubelet_volume_stats_available_bytes`, which tells you how full a PersistentVolume is. The API server exposes `apiserver_*` metrics about itself. Neither belongs to the four above, and alert rules use both.\\n\\n## Do you need kube-state-metrics, metrics-server or both?\\n\\nChoose by the consumer of the data:\\n\\n- **HPA based on Pod CPU or memory, or `kubectl top`:** metrics-server provides the Resource Metrics API for this path. kube-state-metrics does not replace it. An HPA based on custom or external metrics can use a different adapter.\\n- **Alerts about desired state:** use kube-state-metrics for unavailable Deployment replicas, failed Jobs, restart counts and Pod waiting reasons. Scrape its `/metrics` endpoint into Prometheus or a compatible store.\\n- **Container CPU and memory dashboards or alerts:** scrape the kubelet\'s container resource metrics, commonly `/metrics/cadvisor`, into the monitoring store. kube-state-metrics supplies requests and limits to compare the usage with.\\n- **Host disk, network and operating-system checks:** use node_exporter on each node.\\n\\nIn a cluster that uses CPU-based autoscaling and Prometheus alerting, these are complementary data paths. You do not have to route metrics-server through Prometheus, or install kube-state-metrics just to make `kubectl top` work. The [kube-state-metrics README](https://github.com/kubernetes/kube-state-metrics/blob/main/README.md) and [Kubernetes resource metrics pipeline](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/resource-metrics-pipeline/) explain the distinction.\\n\\n## kube-state-metrics: what the API server believes\\n\\nkube-state-metrics listens to the API server and turns objects into metrics. Its README calls this \\"the health of the various objects inside\\" the cluster, as opposed to the health of the cluster components themselves. It exposes the values as the API returns them. It does not apply the heuristics `kubectl` uses, which is why its numbers sometimes differ from what `kubectl get` prints.\\n\\nThat makes it the right source for questions about intent:\\n\\n- Does the Deployment have the replicas it asked for? `kube_deployment_spec_replicas` vs `kube_deployment_status_replicas_available`.\\n- Is a container stuck in `CrashLoopBackOff`? `kube_pod_container_status_waiting_reason`.\\n- Why did the last run end? `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason`, where `reason=\\"OOMKilled\\"` is the one you usually want.\\n- Is a node `NotReady`? `kube_node_status_condition`.\\n\\nIt tells you nothing about usage. A pod at 100% of its CPU limit and an idle pod look the same to kube-state-metrics. For usage you need cAdvisor.\\n\\n**The rename that breaks old queries.** kube-state-metrics 2.0 removed the per-resource metrics such as `kube_pod_container_resource_limits_cpu_cores` and `kube_pod_container_resource_requests_memory_bytes`. Their replacements are `kube_pod_container_resource_limits` and `kube_pod_container_resource_requests`, with `resource` and `unit` labels:\\n\\n```promql\\n# kube-state-metrics 1.x (removed in 2.0)\\nkube_pod_container_resource_limits_cpu_cores\\n\\n# kube-state-metrics 2.x\\nkube_pod_container_resource_limits{resource=\\"cpu\\", unit=\\"core\\"}\\n```\\n\\nA query that uses the old name does not fail. It returns an empty result, and an alert rule built on it never fires. We found exactly this in our own Kubernetes template while writing this post: the [container CPU rule](/alert-rules/kubernetes#container-cpu-usage-exceeds-80) still used the 1.x metric name and the old `pod_name` label. It now uses the current names.\\n\\n## cAdvisor: what containers actually use\\n\\ncAdvisor is compiled into the kubelet, so there is nothing extra to deploy. Prometheus scrapes it from each node at `/metrics/cadvisor`. These are the metrics you reach for when a pod is slow or keeps getting killed:\\n\\n- `container_cpu_usage_seconds_total`: CPU time consumed. Take a `rate()` to get cores in use.\\n- `container_memory_working_set_bytes`: the container\'s working-set estimate. It is not the same as RSS or a definitive measurement of unreclaimable memory; active file cache can contribute to it. Use the termination reason as well when diagnosing an OOM kill.\\n- `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` and `container_cpu_cfs_periods_total`: how often the container hit its CPU limit and was paused. The [CPUThrottlingHigh](/alert-rules/kubernetes#cputhrottlinghigh) rule is the ratio of the two.\\n\\nTwo things trip people up:\\n\\n1. **Extra series.** cAdvisor also reports cgroups that are not your application container: a pod-level series with `container=\\"\\"` and, on older Docker-based nodes, the pause container as `container=\\"POD\\"`. Summing without a filter counts the same memory twice. Add `container!=\\"\\"` (and `container!=\\"POD\\"` on old nodes) when you aggregate.\\n2. **Label names changed.** Older Kubernetes examples use `pod_name` and `container_name`. Current kubelet metrics use `pod` and `container`. Grouping by a missing `pod_name` label loses the intended Pod identity; joins that expect the old labels can return no matches or combine the wrong series. Check the labels on the data you actually scrape.\\n\\nUsage becomes useful when you compare it with a limit, and the limit lives in kube-state-metrics. Both sides share the `namespace`, `pod` and `container` labels, so the join is direct:\\n\\n```promql\\nmax by (cluster, namespace, pod, container) (\\n  container_memory_working_set_bytes{container!=\\"\\", container!=\\"POD\\"}\\n)\\n/\\n(\\n  max by (cluster, namespace, pod, container) (\\n    kube_pod_container_resource_limits{resource=\\"memory\\", unit=\\"byte\\"}\\n  ) > 0\\n)\\n> 0.9\\n```\\n\\nBoth sides must identify the same container. Preserve a common `cluster` label when querying multiple clusters; adapt the grouping labels to your setup. The positive-limit filter excludes containers without a positive memory limit, and `max` avoids adding identical duplicate scrape series together. This is a threshold against configured limits, not proof that the workload is about to be OOM-killed. The [cAdvisor metric reference](https://github.com/google/cadvisor/blob/master/docs/storage/prometheus.md) describes the usage metrics.\\n\\n## node_exporter: the machine underneath\\n\\nOn Kubernetes, node_exporter commonly runs as a DaemonSet with access to the host\'s `/proc` and `/sys`. It knows nothing about pods. It reports the operating system: `node_cpu_seconds_total` by mode, `node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes`, `node_filesystem_avail_bytes`, disk I/O and network errors.\\n\\nUse it for the questions Kubernetes does not answer. Is the node\'s root filesystem filling up? Is the clock drifting? Is a NIC dropping packets? Is the kernel OOM killer firing (`node_vmstat_oom_kill`)? Node-level memory includes page cache and every process on the host, so it will not add up to the sum of your containers. That is expected.\\n\\nThe [Linux rule set](/alert-rules/linux) covers the common host checks, including disk-fill prediction with `predict_linear`.\\n\\n## metrics-server: the autoscaling data path\\n\\nmetrics-server collects CPU and memory every 15 seconds from each kubelet\'s `/metrics/resource` endpoint and serves them through the Metrics API. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler reads it, the Vertical Pod Autoscaler reads it, and `kubectl top` reads it.\\n\\nIt keeps recent resource values in memory rather than a queryable monitoring history. Its own `/metrics` endpoint exposes self-monitoring, not a replacement Prometheus feed for every Pod\'s CPU and memory. The [project\'s README](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/blob/master/README.md) says metrics-server is intended for autoscaling rather than forwarding metrics to monitoring solutions. If you want resource data in a monitoring store, scrape the appropriate kubelet endpoints directly.\\n\\nSo when `kubectl top` and a dashboard disagree, check the sampling window, aggregation, endpoint and metric definition before assuming either value is wrong. For the endpoint layout, see the [Kubernetes system metrics guide](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/system-metrics/).\\n\\n## Can metrics-server replace Prometheus?\\n\\nmetrics-server serves the resource API used by autoscaling and `kubectl top`. Prometheus scrapes and stores time series for historical queries and rule evaluation. Installing one does not provide the other one\'s consumer APIs or operating model.\\n\\nIf you are choosing where the alert rules themselves should run, [Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager](/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager) compares evaluation and notification ownership. The metric sources in this article can remain the same whichever evaluation path you choose.\\n\\n## Which component answers your question\\n\\n| Question | Metric | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Is the pod restarting? | `kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total` | kube-state-metrics |\\n| Was it OOM-killed? | `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason{reason=\\"OOMKilled\\"}` | kube-state-metrics |\\n| Is a container close to its memory limit? | `container_memory_working_set_bytes` \xf7 `kube_pod_container_resource_limits{resource=\\"memory\\"}` | cAdvisor + kube-state-metrics |\\n| Is it being CPU-throttled? | `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` \xf7 `container_cpu_cfs_periods_total` | cAdvisor |\\n| Does the Deployment have the replicas it wants? | `kube_deployment_status_replicas_available` vs `kube_deployment_spec_replicas` | kube-state-metrics |\\n| Is a node `NotReady`? | `kube_node_status_condition{condition=\\"Ready\\"}` | kube-state-metrics |\\n| Is a node\'s disk filling up? | `node_filesystem_avail_bytes` | node_exporter |\\n| Is a PersistentVolume filling up? | `kubelet_volume_stats_available_bytes` | kubelet |\\n| How much CPU is the pod using right now? | `kubectl top pod` | metrics-server |\\n\\n## Ready-made rules, sorted by source\\n\\nMost of the standard Kubernetes alerts read kube-state-metrics. Each of these pages explains what the alert means, its common causes and the steps to fix it, and lists the metrics it uses:\\n\\n- kube-state-metrics: [KubePodCrashLooping](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubepodcrashlooping), [KubePodNotReady](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubepodnotready), [KubeDeploymentReplicasMismatch](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubedeploymentreplicasmismatch), [KubeNodeNotReady](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubenodenotready), [KubeJobFailed](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubejobfailed), [KubeHpaMaxedOut](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubehpamaxedout)\\n- kubelet: [KubePersistentVolumeFillingUp](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubepersistentvolumefillingup)\\n- cAdvisor: [CPUThrottlingHigh](/alert-rules/kubernetes#cputhrottlinghigh)\\n- API server: [KubeAPIErrorBudgetBurn](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubeapierrorbudgetburn), [KubeClientCertificateExpiration](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubeclientcertificateexpiration)\\n\\nThe full [Kubernetes rule set](/alert-rules/kubernetes) is available as a Prometheus rules file you can download and load with `rule_files`.\\n\\n## With Nightingale\\n\\nNightingale does not replace any of these four components. It queries the store where their metrics already land, such as Prometheus or VictoriaMetrics, and runs alert rules against it. The [Kubernetes integration](/integrations/kubernetes) ships the rules above, each with its runbook attached, and you can [import them into a business group](/docs/data/integrations/import) in one step."},{"id":"mcp-server-for-alerting","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mcp-server-for-alerting","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-mcp-server-design/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-mcp-server-design/index.md","title":"MCP server for alerting: six design decisions","description":"How Nightingale\'s built-in MCP server handles permissions, write access and audit: RBAC inherited from the token, write tools unregistered by default, no delete tools, and one caveat.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"AI and MCP","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai","description":"AI assistants, MCP servers and how they touch production systems."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."}],"readingTime":6.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-server-for-alerting","title":"MCP server for alerting: six design decisions","description":"How Nightingale\'s built-in MCP server handles permissions, write access and audit: RBAC inherited from the token, write tools unregistered by default, no delete tools, and one caveat.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["ai","alerting"],"image":"/img/blog/mcp-server-design.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"kube-state-metrics vs metrics-server: which should you use?","permalink":"/blog/kube-state-metrics-vs-metrics-server-vs-cadvisor-vs-node-exporter"},"nextItem":{"title":"Prometheus high availability: storage vs alerting","permalink":"/blog/prometheus-high-availability"}},"content":"![An AI client asking which alerts are firing, connected with a user\'s token to Nightingale\'s MCP endpoint](/img/blog/mcp-server-design.webp)\\n\\nSince v9, the Nightingale process is also an MCP server. Claude Code, Cursor or any other MCP client can connect to `/mcp` and ask which alerts are firing, which rules are noisiest, or what a metric did over the last hour.\\n\\nThe tool list was the easy part. The hard questions were about trust. Whose permissions does the AI client have? What can it change? How do you find out afterwards what it did? This post walks through the six decisions we made, and one caveat we haven\'t solved.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nWhat this is not: an autonomous agent that resolves incidents on its own. It is a way for a model to read, and with explicit opt-in to change, an alerting system with the same permissions as the person who connected it.\\n\\n## 1. No separate server: the endpoint lives in the process\\n\\nThere is nothing extra to deploy. `POST /mcp` is served by the same `n9e` process that serves the UI and the API, and it is on by default.\\n\\nIt uses MCP\'s Streamable HTTP transport in **stateless** mode. Every POST is self-contained. The server returns an `Mcp-Session-Id` on initialize but never validates it, and `GET /mcp` returns 405 because there is no standalone event stream. The practical benefit: several Nightingale instances behind a load balancer need no sticky sessions. Any instance can answer any request.\\n\\n## 2. A tool call is an internal API call\\n\\nWhen a client calls `list_alert_rules`, the MCP server does not read the database. It dispatches the call, in-process, onto Nightingale\'s own HTTP route (`/api/n9e/busi-group/{gid}/alert-rules`), carrying the caller\'s token through the full middleware chain: authenticate, resolve the user, check the permission point, check the business group.\\n\\nSo the answer to \\"can the AI get around RBAC?\\" is structural: no. It walks the same path as a click in the UI. There is no separate permission model for AI to configure, audit and keep in sync.\\n\\nThe consequence is that **a client has exactly the permissions of its token\'s owner**. To limit what the AI can see, you give it a limited account: a dedicated user with the `Guest` or `Standard` role, in a team that can read only the business groups you choose. `list_busi_groups` returns only groups that user can reach, so the model can only ever pick from that set. Do not give the AI account the `Admin` role. Admin bypasses the business-group layer.\\n\\nOAuth tokens get one more restriction. A token issued through OAuth works on `/mcp` and `/a2a` only; the rest of the API rejects it.\\n\\n## 3. Write tools are not registered, not just refused\\n\\nThere are 74 tools. 42 read and 32 write. By default only the 42 read tools exist. The write tools sit in a separate registration list that is skipped unless you set:\\n\\n```toml\\n[HTTP.A2A]\\nMCPEnableWriteTools = true\\n```\\n\\nWe chose \\"not registered\\" over \\"registered and refused\\" on purpose. A model plans with the tools it can see. If write tools appear in `tools/list` and fail at call time, the model keeps trying them, or tries to work around the error. If they are absent, the model never considers them.\\n\\nThis gate sits outside RBAC. Even if a client is handed root\'s token by mistake, under the default config it cannot change anything. Every tool also carries MCP\'s `readOnlyHint` annotation for clients that use it, and it always agrees with the registration list.\\n\\n## 4. No delete tools at all\\n\\nNone of the 74 tools deletes anything. \\"Write\\" means create and update. Deleting a rule, a user or a business group is not reachable through MCP.\\n\\nThis changes the worst case from \\"gone\\" to \\"changed wrongly\\", which is easier to notice and to fix. It does not make write tools harmless. Some of them are among the most powerful operations in the product:\\n\\n| Tool | Why it is dangerous |\\n|---|---|\\n| `reset_user_password` | Resets any user\'s password |\\n| `create_user`, `update_user_profile` | Assign roles as a side effect, which is privilege escalation |\\n| `bind_role_operations` | Replaces a role\'s whole permission set; a short payload silently removes permissions |\\n| `create_notify_channel`, `update_notify_channel` | A webhook channel sends alert content to any address it is given |\\n| `upsert_datasource` | Stores data source credentials and probes the address it is given |\\n| `set_dashboard_public` | Makes a dashboard readable without login |\\n| `toggle_alert_rules`, `set_datasource_status`, `create_mute` | Create blind spots: rules disabled, a data source off, alerts silenced |\\n\\n## 5. Toolsets are an allowlist, and typos don\'t fall back\\n\\nThe 74 tools are grouped into 13 toolsets: `alerts`, `targets`, `datasource`, `mutes`, `busi_groups`, `notify_rules`, `alert_subscribes`, `event_pipelines`, `users`, `metrics`, `logs`, `dashboards` and `roles`. An empty list exposes all of them; a list exposes only those named:\\n\\n```toml\\n[HTTP.A2A]\\nMCPToolsets = [\\"alerts\\", \\"busi_groups\\", \\"targets\\", \\"metrics\\"]\\n```\\n\\nA misspelled name is dropped with a warning in the log. It does **not** fall back to \\"everything\\". A list made entirely of typos exposes zero tools, not 74. Failing closed is less convenient and much safer.\\n\\nWork backwards from what you want the AI to do:\\n\\n| Use | Toolsets |\\n|---|---|\\n| On-call triage: what is firing, which rule, who owns it | `alerts`, `busi_groups`, `targets` |\\n| Plus \\"explain this spike\\" | add `metrics` |\\n| Noise review: noisiest rules, mutes, subscriptions | add `mutes`, `alert_subscribes` |\\n| Letting the AI change configuration | turn on writes, and remove `users` and `roles` first |\\n\\n## 6. Every call is attributable\\n\\nTwo trails answer \\"who changed this, and was it the AI?\\":\\n\\n- **The object itself.** A rule, mute or dashboard changed over MCP records the token owner\'s username in `update_by`, exactly as if they had edited it in the UI.\\n- **The request log.** Every `/mcp` request writes a start line with the full request body (tool name and arguments) and a done line with the user and status code, joined by a `trace_id`:\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  grep \'\\"method\\":\\"tools/call\\"\' n9e.log | grep -oE \'\\"name\\":\\"[a-z_]+\\"\' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\\n  ```\\n\\n  Oversized bodies are truncated, and the log line says so.\\n\\nA dedicated account per client makes both trails useful. If every client shares one token, `update_by` tells you nothing.\\n\\n## The caveat: `query_logs` and SQL data sources\\n\\nOne read-only tool deserves care. `query_logs` in the `logs` toolset passes the query body to Nightingale\'s log query endpoint, which dispatches on the data source type. If you have SQL data sources registered (MySQL, PostgreSQL, ClickHouse, Doris or TDengine), the SQL runs as written.\\n\\nThere is a read-only check on that path. It is a keyword blocklist that rejects statements containing `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `DROP`, `ALTER`, `CREATE`, `TRUNCATE`, `REPLACE`, `GRANT` or `REVOKE`. That stops the obvious cases. It is not a SQL parser, so treat it as a second line of defence rather than a guarantee. If you have SQL data sources, leave `logs` out of `MCPToolsets`, or give the AI account a role that cannot reach those data sources. Registering them with read-only database credentials is a sensible extra layer.\\n\\n## MCP or the built-in assistant?\\n\\nNightingale also has a built-in assistant in the UI. For configuration changes it is the safer of the two: its update operations are two-phase. The first call computes the change and shows a confirmation card, and nothing is written until a person confirms. MCP has no such step. A write tool writes on its first call.\\n\\nSo a reasonable split is: use an MCP client for reading and triage, where its own model and tools shine, and use the built-in assistant when something needs to change.\\n\\n## Try it\\n\\nCreate a token for a dedicated, least-privileged user under **Profile \u2192 Token management**, then add Nightingale to your MCP client:\\n\\n```json title=\\"~/.claude.json\\"\\n{\\n  \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n    \\"nightingale\\": {\\n      \\"type\\": \\"http\\",\\n      \\"url\\": \\"http://127.0.0.1:17000/mcp\\",\\n      \\"headers\\": { \\"X-User-Token\\": \\"<token of the AI account>\\" }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen ask something you can check: *\\"Which alerts are firing right now? Group them by severity.\\"* or *\\"List every business group I can see.\\"* The second one is also a quick test that the account is scoped the way you intended.\\n\\nMore detail is in the docs: [Enable the MCP endpoint](/docs/ai/mcp-endpoint), [Permission inheritance and RBAC](/docs/ai/permissions), [Read and write toolsets](/docs/ai/toolsets) and [Enable write tools safely](/docs/ai/write-tools)."},{"id":"prometheus-high-availability","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/prometheus-high-availability","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-prometheus-high-availability/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-prometheus-high-availability/index.md","title":"Prometheus high availability: storage vs alerting","description":"Running two Prometheus replicas solves half of high availability. How Alertmanager clustering, replica labels and Thanos, Mimir or VictoriaMetrics deduplication fit together.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."},{"inline":false,"label":"High availability","permalink":"/blog/tags/high-availability","description":"Keeping monitoring and alerting up when parts of it fail."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."}],"readingTime":6.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"prometheus-high-availability","title":"Prometheus high availability: storage vs alerting","description":"Running two Prometheus replicas solves half of high availability. How Alertmanager clustering, replica labels and Thanos, Mimir or VictoriaMetrics deduplication fit together.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["prometheus","high-availability","alerting"],"image":"/img/blog/prometheus-high-availability.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MCP server for alerting: six design decisions","permalink":"/blog/mcp-server-for-alerting"},"nextItem":{"title":"Symptom-based alerting with Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/symptom-based-alerting"}},"content":"![Two Prometheus replicas writing to deduplicated storage and sending alerts to a clustered Alertmanager](/img/blog/prometheus-high-availability.webp)\\n\\nAsk how to make Prometheus highly available and the usual answer is \\"run two of them\\". That is correct, and it is only half the job. Two replicas give you two copies of every alert and two slightly different copies of every time series. Each needs its own fix, and the fixes don\'t share a configuration.\\n\\nThis post separates the two problems: keeping alerts flowing exactly once, and keeping the data queryable without gaps. It ends with a checklist.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## How Prometheus does HA\\n\\nPrometheus has no clustering. High availability means running two (or more) identical servers. Each one scrapes the same targets, evaluates the same rules and keeps its own local copy of the data. They don\'t talk to each other.\\n\\nThe copies are never identical. Each replica scrapes at its own moment, so the timestamps differ. When one replica restarts, it has a gap that the other doesn\'t. That is fine for alerting, and a problem for dashboards.\\n\\nThe usual convention is to give each replica an external label that says which one it is:\\n\\n```yaml\\n# prometheus-a.yml (prometheus-b.yml says replica: b)\\nglobal:\\n  external_labels:\\n    cluster: prod-eu\\n    replica: a\\n```\\n\\n## Problem 1: alerts should arrive once, even when a replica dies\\n\\nBoth replicas evaluate every rule, so both send every alert. Deduplication happens in Alertmanager.\\n\\n**Cluster the Alertmanagers.** Alertmanager instances gossip with each other. You start each one with `--cluster.peer` pointing at the others, and they share notification state and silences. When two replicas send the same alert, the cluster sends one notification.\\n\\n**Point every Prometheus at every Alertmanager.** The Alertmanager README is explicit: do not load-balance traffic between Prometheus and its Alertmanagers. Give each Prometheus the full list, because every alert has to reach every instance:\\n\\n```yaml\\nalerting:\\n  alertmanagers:\\n    - static_configs:\\n        - targets: [\'am-0:9093\', \'am-1:9093\', \'am-2:9093\']\\n```\\n\\n**Drop the replica label from alerts.** Alerts carry the external labels. If replica A sends `{alertname=\\"X\\", replica=\\"a\\"}` and replica B sends `{alertname=\\"X\\", replica=\\"b\\"}`, Alertmanager sees two different alerts and notifies twice. Strip the label on the way out:\\n\\n```yaml\\nalerting:\\n  alert_relabel_configs:\\n    - action: labeldrop\\n      regex: replica\\n```\\n\\nThe Prometheus Operator does this for you with its own replica label. Hand-built setups often miss it, and the symptom is that every page arrives twice.\\n\\nWith these three in place, alerting HA works well. If replica A dies, replica B was evaluating the same rules all along, so there is no gap in detection. If an Alertmanager dies, Prometheus is still sending to the others.\\n\\n## Problem 2: dashboards and queries should not show gaps\\n\\nThis is the half that \\"run two\\" does not solve. Point Grafana at replica A, and you see replica A\'s gaps. Put a load balancer in front of both, and consecutive refreshes can hit different replicas and draw slightly different lines.\\n\\nThe fix is a layer that knows the two replicas hold the same data and merges them. The three common options handle the replica label in different ways, and that difference matters:\\n\\n| Store | How it deduplicates | What the replicas\' labels must look like |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Thanos** | Thanos Querier merges series that differ only in the label named by `--query.replica-label` | Different: each replica has its own `replica` value |\\n| **Grafana Mimir** | The HA tracker elects a leader replica per cluster, keeps its samples and drops the rest. It switches leader if the current one sends nothing for 30 seconds by default | Different: `cluster` plus a `__replica__` label by default |\\n| **VictoriaMetrics** | `-dedup.minScrapeInterval` keeps one sample per series per interval. Set it equal to your `scrape_interval` | **Identical**: the docs require the HA pair to write the same series, so no per-replica label |\\n\\nSo a setup moved from Thanos to VictoriaMetrics needs its replica label removed, and a setup moved the other way needs one added. Mixing the conventions gives you either doubled series or a store that silently keeps one replica.\\n\\nLong-term retention is a separate decision from HA. Thanos, Mimir and VictoriaMetrics also keep data longer than a local Prometheus. But you can have deduplicated HA with short retention, or long retention with a single replica. Decide each one on its own.\\n\\n## Scaling is not HA\\n\\nFederation, functional sharding (one Prometheus per team or per cluster) and `hashmod` sharding spread load. They don\'t add redundancy: each shard is still a single point of failure until you run it as a pair. If you shard, every shard needs the replica treatment above.\\n\\n## Where a separate alerting engine changes the picture\\n\\nThe [Grafana Alerting vs Alertmanager guide](/blog/grafana-alerting-vs-alertmanager) explains rule ownership and how the two can share an external notification path.\\n\\nEverything above assumes Prometheus evaluates the rules. Some setups move evaluation out of Prometheus: Grafana Alerting, vmalert, Thanos Ruler, or [Nightingale](/docs/install/high-availability). Then storage HA and alerting HA become two different systems.\\n\\nNightingale takes a different approach from a Prometheus pair, with a different trade-off:\\n\\n- **It queries one deduplicated data source.** Prometheus behind Thanos Querier, VictoriaMetrics or Mimir. The storage-side HA from Problem 2 is still needed, and Nightingale doesn\'t replace it.\\n- **Rules are shared out, not duplicated.** Several `n9e` instances share one MySQL and one Redis. Each live instance sends a heartbeat every second. The live instances form a consistent hash ring per data source, and each rule runs on exactly one instance. One evaluation means one event, so no Alertmanager-style deduplication is needed.\\n- **The trade-off is a failover gap.** An instance counts as dead after 30 seconds without a heartbeat, and only then does its share of rules move to another instance. A freshly started instance also waits 30 seconds before evaluating. A Prometheus pair has no such gap, because the other replica was evaluating all along.\\n- **Shared dependencies need their own HA.** If MySQL is down, everything is down. If Redis is unreachable at startup, the process exits. Running several `n9e` instances protects against losing an `n9e` process, and nothing else.\\n\\nNeither design is better in general. Duplicate evaluation with downstream deduplication gives zero-gap failover and costs you a deduplication layer that must be configured exactly right. Single evaluation with a hash ring avoids duplicates by construction and costs you up to 30 seconds of blindness for the rules on a failed instance. The details are in [High availability and failure domains](/docs/operations/high-availability).\\n\\n## Checklist\\n\\n**Alerting**\\n\\n- [ ] Two or more Prometheus replicas with identical rule files.\\n- [ ] Alertmanager clustered with `--cluster.peer`; the cluster port reachable between instances.\\n- [ ] Every Prometheus lists every Alertmanager. No load balancer in between.\\n- [ ] The replica label dropped in `alert_relabel_configs`.\\n- [ ] Tested: stop one replica and one Alertmanager, fire a test alert, and get exactly one notification.\\n\\n**Storage and queries**\\n\\n- [ ] A deduplicating layer: Thanos Querier, Mimir\'s HA tracker or VictoriaMetrics deduplication.\\n- [ ] Replica labels set the way that layer expects. Thanos and Mimir need them to differ; VictoriaMetrics needs them identical.\\n- [ ] Dashboards and alert rules query the deduplicated endpoint, not a single replica.\\n- [ ] Tested: restart one replica and check that a dashboard over that window shows no gap.\\n\\n**Everything**\\n\\n- [ ] The monitoring system is monitored from outside itself. A dead-man\'s-switch alert that always fires, and pages when it *stops* arriving, catches the case where the whole alerting path is down.\\n\\nThe last item is the one most often skipped. Every check above runs inside the system it is checking."},{"id":"symptom-based-alerting","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/symptom-based-alerting","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-symptom-based-alerting/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-symptom-based-alerting/index.md","title":"Symptom-based alerting with Prometheus","description":"Symptom-based vs cause-based alerting with real Prometheus rules: error ratio, latency and probe alerts that page, and where CPU, disk and certificate alerts still belong.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."},{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."}],"readingTime":6.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"symptom-based-alerting","title":"Symptom-based alerting with Prometheus","description":"Symptom-based vs cause-based alerting with real Prometheus rules: error ratio, latency and probe alerts that page, and where CPU, disk and certificate alerts still belong.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["alerting","prometheus"],"image":"/img/blog/symptom-based-alerting.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Prometheus high availability: storage vs alerting","permalink":"/blog/prometheus-high-availability"},"nextItem":{"title":"Migrating from Zabbix to Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/migrate-zabbix-to-prometheus"}},"content":"![A greyed-out CPU alert next to a highlighted checkout error-rate alert](/img/blog/symptom-based-alerting.webp)\\n\\nAt 3 a.m. an alert says CPU is at 92% on `db-3`. The on-call engineer opens the dashboards. Checkout is fast, errors are flat, and nobody is affected. The alert was true and useless.\\n\\nA week later, checkout fails for 20 minutes because a dependency returns errors. CPU is fine, memory is fine, every host looks healthy. No alert fires.\\n\\nBoth failures have the same root cause: the rules watch *causes*, not *symptoms*. This post explains the difference, shows what symptom rules look like in PromQL, and explains where cause alerts still earn their place.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Symptoms and causes\\n\\nA **symptom** is what users experience: requests failing, pages loading slowly, a service unreachable, data arriving late. A **cause** is why: a saturated CPU, a full disk, a crashed pod, a slow query.\\n\\nThe idea comes from Google\'s SRE book. Chapter 6, \\"Monitoring Distributed Systems\\", argues that paging should be driven by symptoms, and proposes four golden signals to watch on every user-facing system: **latency, traffic, errors and saturation**. Rob Ewaschuk\'s essay \\"My Philosophy on Alerting\\", written while he was an SRE at Google, makes the same case in more detail.\\n\\nCause-based paging fails in two directions:\\n\\n- **Causes without symptoms create noise.** High CPU, a pod restart, one replica out of three down. Systems absorb these all the time. Each page that turns out to be harmless teaches people to ignore the next one.\\n- **Symptoms without a known cause create silence.** You can only write cause rules for failures you have imagined. A new failure mode, a dependency outage or a bad deploy that leaves every resource metric normal goes unnoticed.\\n\\nA symptom rule catches both the failures you predicted and the ones you didn\'t, because it watches the outcome instead of the mechanism.\\n\\n## What symptom rules look like\\n\\nThe metric names below follow common conventions. Yours will depend on your instrumentation library, ingress controller or service mesh.\\n\\n**Errors: the share of requests that fail.**\\n\\n```promql\\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"checkout\\", code=~\\"5..\\"}[5m]))\\n  /\\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"checkout\\"}[5m]))\\n> 0.02\\n```\\n\\nUse a ratio, not a count. 50 errors a minute is a disaster at 100 requests and noise at 100,000.\\n\\n**Latency: how slow the slow requests are.**\\n\\n```promql\\nhistogram_quantile(0.99,\\n  sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job=\\"checkout\\"}[5m]))\\n) > 1\\n```\\n\\nAlert on a high percentile, not the average. An average of 200 ms can hide one request in fifty taking ten seconds.\\n\\n**Availability from outside.** A probe that calls the service the way a user would catches DNS, TLS, load balancer and network failures that internal metrics never see. With the Prometheus blackbox exporter that is `probe_success == 0`. Our [Linux rule set](/alert-rules/linux) includes an \\"HTTP probe failed\\" rule for Categraf\'s HTTP checks.\\n\\n**Freshness and throughput** for things that are not request-driven. Did the nightly job run? Is the queue draining?\\n\\n```promql\\ntime() - batch_job_last_success_timestamp_seconds{job=\\"nightly-export\\"} > 26 * 3600\\n```\\n\\n### Three details that make symptom rules work\\n\\n1. **Measure where users are.** The ingress or load balancer sees every request, including the ones that never reached a healthy pod. Per-pod error rates miss requests that were dropped before reaching any pod.\\n2. **Aggregate across instances.** `sum(...)` over the whole service, not one alert per pod. Users don\'t care which replica failed them.\\n3. **Guard against low traffic.** At three requests a minute, one failure is a 33% error ratio. Add a minimum volume:\\n\\n   ```promql\\n   (\\n     sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"checkout\\", code=~\\"5..\\"}[5m]))\\n       / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"checkout\\"}[5m]))\\n   ) > 0.02\\n   and\\n   sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"checkout\\"}[5m])) > 1\\n   ```\\n\\n## Where cause alerts still belong\\n\\n\\"Alert on symptoms\\" doesn\'t mean \\"delete every cause alert\\". Some causes deserve a notification. They just shouldn\'t wake anyone up.\\n\\n**Deadlines.** A disk that will be full in 24 hours, or a certificate that expires in 7 days, is a symptom waiting to happen, with a known date. Alert on these during working hours, at warning severity. The Linux library has a `predict_linear` rule for disks; the Kubernetes library has [certificate expiry](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubeclientcertificateexpiration) rules.\\n\\n**Lost redundancy.** One replica of three is down. Users are fine, but the next failure will hurt them. That is worth a ticket today, not a page tonight.\\n\\n**The monitoring itself.** If a scrape target or collector stops reporting, every rule that depends on it looks healthy. `up == 0`, or a dead-man\'s-switch alert that always fires, is a cause alert you need from day one.\\n\\n**Explanation.** Everything else, such as CPU, memory, GC pauses and connection counts, belongs on a dashboard or as a low-severity alert that someone looks at while investigating a symptom. The Kubernetes rules in our library follow this: \\"Node CPU usage exceeds 90%\\" ships at **info** severity, while the symptom-shaped rules such as [KubeAPIErrorBudgetBurn](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubeapierrorbudgetburn) go up to critical.\\n\\n## Connecting symptoms to causes\\n\\nA symptom alert tells you that something is wrong. The responder then needs to know where to look. Two ways to close that gap:\\n\\n- **Put the causes in the runbook.** Every symptom alert should link to a list of likely causes and the commands that confirm each one. Each page in our [alert rule library](/alert-rules) follows that shape: what the alert means, common causes, then the steps to fix it. [KubePodCrashLooping](/alert-rules/kubernetes/kubepodcrashlooping) is a typical example.\\n- **Mute symptoms while a known cause is active.** If a whole site loses its uplink, every service there reports errors. Alertmanager\'s `inhibit_rules`, or an equivalent, can mute the dependent alerts while the upstream alert is firing. That needs a shared label such as `site` on both.\\n\\n## A worked example\\n\\nHere is a rule set for a checkout service built on these ideas:\\n\\n| Rule | Kind | Severity | Who hears about it |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Error ratio above 2% for 5 minutes | Symptom | Critical | Page |\\n| p99 latency above 1 s for 10 minutes | Symptom | Critical | Page |\\n| External probe failing from 2 or more locations | Symptom | Critical | Page |\\n| Error ratio above 0.5% for 30 minutes | Symptom, slow burn | Warning | Team channel |\\n| Disk full within 24 hours | Cause with a deadline | Warning | Team channel |\\n| One of three replicas down for 15 minutes | Lost redundancy | Warning | Team channel |\\n| Scrape target down | Monitoring | Warning | Team channel |\\n| CPU, memory, GC, DB connections | Cause | Info or dashboard | Nobody, until someone investigates |\\n\\nThree rules can page, and all three describe something a customer would notice.\\n\\n## Auditing a rule set you already have\\n\\nFor each rule that can page, ask: *if this fires and users are fine, would anyone do anything?* If the answer is no, lower its severity or move it to a dashboard.\\n\\nThen look at the data. List the rules that fired most often in the last month and how many of those firings led to an action. Also list the rules that never fired in a year. They are usually broken rather than lucky: a renamed metric, a label that changed, a threshold that cannot be reached. Nightingale\'s built-in assistant can answer both questions directly (\\"which rules fired most often in the last 7 days, and which have never fired?\\"); see [Common investigation workflows](/docs/ai/workflows#find-the-noise).\\n\\nFor the rules that stay, the [rule design best practices](/docs/alerting/best-practices) page covers naming, labels and how much one rule should cover."},{"id":"migrate-zabbix-to-prometheus","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/migrate-zabbix-to-prometheus","editUrl":"https://github.com/n9e/n9e.github.io/edit/main/blog/2026-11-01-zabbix-to-prometheus/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-zabbix-to-prometheus/index.md","title":"Migrating from Zabbix to Prometheus","description":"A concept-by-concept map from Zabbix to Prometheus: hosts, items, triggers, LLD, dependencies, maintenance. Plus the gaps with no equivalent and a migration order that never turns Zabbix off.","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Migration","permalink":"/blog/tags/migration","description":"Moving between monitoring and alerting systems."},{"inline":false,"label":"Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/tags/prometheus","description":"Prometheus metrics, rules and operations."},{"inline":false,"label":"Alerting","permalink":"/blog/tags/alerting","description":"Designing alert rules that page for the right reasons."}],"readingTime":6.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Eric Qin","key":"eric-qin","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"migrate-zabbix-to-prometheus","title":"Migrating from Zabbix to Prometheus","description":"A concept-by-concept map from Zabbix to Prometheus: hosts, items, triggers, LLD, dependencies, maintenance. Plus the gaps with no equivalent and a migration order that never turns Zabbix off.","authors":["eric-qin"],"tags":["migration","prometheus","alerting"],"image":"/img/blog/zabbix-to-prometheus.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Symptom-based alerting with Prometheus","permalink":"/blog/symptom-based-alerting"}},"content":"![From Zabbix hosts, items and triggers to Prometheus targets, metrics and one alert rule covering every host](/img/blog/zabbix-to-prometheus.webp)\\n\\nMoving from Zabbix to Prometheus is not a data migration. There is no export file that turns into rule files, and no tool that converts triggers into PromQL. The two systems model monitoring differently. Most of the work is translating concepts, then rebuilding a smaller set of rules on top of them.\\n\\nThis post maps each Zabbix concept to its Prometheus counterpart, lists the parts that have no equivalent, and suggests an order of work that keeps Zabbix running until the end.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The difference that explains everything else\\n\\n**Zabbix is host-centric.** You create a host, attach templates, and the templates define items (what to collect) and triggers (when to alert) for that host. 40 database servers with one template give you 40 copies of each trigger.\\n\\n**Prometheus is query-centric.** Exporters expose metrics, Prometheus scrapes them, and an alert rule is a query. A rule matches every series the query returns. One rule covers all 40 servers, and the 41st server is covered as soon as it is scraped.\\n\\nMost of the translation follows from this. You rarely port triggers one by one; you merge them.\\n\\n## Concept map\\n\\n### Collection and storage\\n\\n| Zabbix | Prometheus | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Zabbix server and database | Prometheus server and its local TSDB | Local retention is short by default (15 days). Long-term storage is a separate component |\\n| Zabbix agent | Exporters: node_exporter for hosts, mysqld_exporter for MySQL and so on | Prometheus *pulls* over HTTP; the agent\'s active checks *push*. Targets must be reachable from Prometheus |\\n| Zabbix proxy | A Prometheus server per site, or Prometheus in agent mode with `remote_write` | Agent mode scrapes and forwards without evaluating rules locally |\\n| Item | A metric name plus labels | Which metrics exist is decided by the exporter, not by the monitoring server |\\n| Item key `vfs.fs.size[/,pfree]` | `node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=\\"/\\"}` \xf7 `node_filesystem_size_bytes{mountpoint=\\"/\\"}` | Percentages are usually computed in the query |\\n| Calculated item | Recording rule | |\\n| History and trends | Raw samples; downsampling only in long-term stores such as Thanos | There is no built-in hourly min/avg/max like Zabbix trends |\\n| Text and log items | None | Prometheus stores numbers only. Logs go to Loki, Elasticsearch or similar |\\n\\n### Hosts, templates and discovery\\n\\n| Zabbix | Prometheus | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Host | A scrape target, identified by the `instance` label | |\\n| Host group | Labels such as `team` or `env`, added through relabeling | Groups become labels you can filter on |\\n| Template | An exporter, a rule file and a dashboard | The community equivalent of a template collection is a monitoring mixin |\\n| Low-level discovery (LLD) | Service discovery for targets; labels for things on a target | New filesystems or interfaces appear as new series automatically. No discovery rule needed |\\n| User macros `{$THRESHOLD}` | Hard-coded thresholds, or a threshold metric joined in the query | Per-host overrides need a pattern, see below |\\n\\n### Triggers and alerting\\n\\n| Zabbix | Prometheus | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Trigger | Alerting rule: a PromQL `expr` plus `for` | One rule, many series |\\n| Trigger severity (six levels) | A `severity` label | A convention, not a built-in. Most teams use three: critical, warning, info |\\n| Recovery expression, hysteresis | No direct equivalent | `keep_firing_for` delays recovery; true hysteresis needs a query trick |\\n| `nodata()` | `absent()`, `absent_over_time()`, or `up == 0` | `absent()` only works for series you name explicitly |\\n| Trigger dependencies | Alertmanager `inhibit_rules` | Inhibition mutes target alerts while a source alert fires, matched on labels |\\n| Event correlation | None in Prometheus or Alertmanager | |\\n\\n### Notification\\n\\n| Zabbix | Prometheus | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Action and operations | Alertmanager routes and receivers | Routing matches on labels |\\n| Media type | Receiver integration: email, Slack, webhook and others | |\\n| Escalation steps | Not built in | Alertmanager repeats notifications (`repeat_interval`) but has no escalation chain. That is an on-call tool\'s job |\\n| Maintenance period | Silences, plus `mute_time_intervals` for recurring windows | Silences are ad hoc; time intervals cover the weekly window |\\n| Acknowledgement | None in Alertmanager | Usually handled in the on-call or chat tool |\\n\\n## The parts with no equivalent\\n\\nPlan for these before you start. They are where migrations stall.\\n\\n1. **Escalations.** Zabbix can notify the on-call engineer, then the team lead after 30 minutes, then the manager. Alertmanager cannot. If you rely on escalation, you need an on-call tool behind Alertmanager.\\n2. **Per-host thresholds.** In Zabbix you override `{$DISK_PFREE_MIN}` on one host. In Prometheus a threshold is part of the query. The common workaround is a small metric that carries thresholds, joined in the rule:\\n\\n   ```promql\\n   (node_filesystem_avail_bytes / node_filesystem_size_bytes) * 100\\n     < on(instance) group_left()\\n   disk_free_threshold_percent\\n   ```\\n\\n   Or keep a default rule and split the exceptions out by label: `disk_profile=\\"large\\"` gets its own rule.\\n3. **SNMP traps.** The SNMP exporter polls; it does not receive traps. Traps need a separate receiver that turns them into metrics or logs.\\n4. **Text and log items.** If Zabbix watches log files or string values, those move to a log system, not Prometheus.\\n5. **Trigger dependencies, partly.** `inhibit_rules` cover the common case of \\"switch down mutes everything behind it\\". They need a label that links the two alerts, such as a shared `site` or `rack`. Zabbix\'s dependency tree has no labels to rely on, so you add them first.\\n\\n## An order that keeps Zabbix running\\n\\nZabbix stays up and keeps paging people until the very last step. Each step can be checked on its own.\\n\\n1. **Deploy exporters next to the Zabbix agents.** Start with one group of hosts. Leave the agents installed. *Check*: the targets are `up` in Prometheus.\\n2. **Pick the storage you will keep.** A single Prometheus for a start; a pair and a long-term store if you need HA or longer retention. Decide now, because changing stores later is a project of its own.\\n3. **Import existing rule sets instead of porting templates.** The community rules for node_exporter, MySQL, Redis, Kubernetes and others cover most of what stock Zabbix templates do. Our [alert rule library](/alert-rules) has them as downloadable rule files, each with a runbook.\\n4. **Port the custom triggers, merged.** List your custom triggers, group them by item and threshold, and write one rule per group. Use labels for the exceptions.\\n5. **Send Prometheus alerts somewhere nobody is paged.** A dedicated channel or mailbox. Compare it with Zabbix every day: fired only in Zabbix (missed rule or metric), fired only in Prometheus (threshold typo, or a Zabbix dependency or maintenance window you forgot), fired in both at different severity.\\n6. **Move notification one channel at a time.** Chat first, then email, then phone. Disable the matching Zabbix action as each channel moves, but keep Zabbix evaluating so the daily comparison continues.\\n7. **Stop Zabbix last.** After a week with no differences, stop the agents. Keep the database read-only for as long as you need the history. History does not migrate.\\n\\n## Zabbix or Prometheus at all?\\n\\nNot every Zabbix setup should move. Zabbix suits fleets of long-lived hosts and network devices, teams that want everything configured in one UI, and environments that need agents to push through firewalls. Prometheus suits environments where targets come and go (containers, autoscaling), where configuration lives in Git, and where software already exposes `/metrics`. Many organisations run both for years: Zabbix for network gear and legacy hosts, Prometheus for Kubernetes and new services.\\n\\n## With Nightingale\\n\\n[Nightingale](/docs/migration/from-zabbix) sits on top of Prometheus or VictoriaMetrics as the alerting layer. It manages rules in a UI with business groups and permissions, and handles notification, muting and subscriptions, so step 6 does not require learning Alertmanager\'s routing tree. It does **not** import Zabbix templates or triggers; the rebuild described above still applies.\\n\\nTwo Nightingale pages go deeper:\\n\\n- [From Zabbix](/docs/migration/from-zabbix): every Zabbix object mapped to a Nightingale one, including the ones with no equivalent.\\n- [Coexistence and rollback plan](/docs/migration/coexistence): the daily comparison and channel-by-channel cutover in detail, with a rollback for each stage.\\n\\nIf you are still choosing, see [Nightingale vs Zabbix](/comparisons/zabbix)."}]}}')}}]);